Note that hashing the `_tsid` at the moment breaks Downsampling when, as part of the bulk request, it tries to write documents to the target index. This happens because in `buildDownsampleDocument` we try to decode the _tsid to extract dimensions and compute the document id in the target index...we would need to use the source/mappings. See


```
// Extract dimension values from _tsid field, so we avoid loading them from doc_values
Map<?, ?> dimensions = (Map<?, ?>) DocValueFormat.TIME_SERIES_ID.format(tsid);
for (Map.Entry<?, ?> e : dimensions.entrySet()) {
    assert e.getValue() != null;
    builder.field((String) e.getKey(), e.getValue());
}
```

In light of @axw's comments and the discussions in https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/99123, I've been thinking about a slightly different way to construct the _tsid.

The guiding principles are:
* Similar time series should be co-located. This implies that the _tsid should contain several parts, going from general to specific.
* The metric names need to be part of the time series.
* The risk of collisions in the _tsid should be very low.

Here's what I ended up with in pseudo-code.
```java
// co-locate time series with the same dimension keys,
// even if the dimension values or metrics they store differ
hash128(sortedDimensions.keys())
  // within time series that have the same dimension keys, co-locate time series with the same metric names,
  // even if the dimension values differ.
  // This makes the metric name(s) part of the identity of a time series
  // and fixes https://github.com/elastic/elasticsearch/issues/99123.
  + hash128(sortedMetricNames) 
  // within time series that have the same set of keys and metric names,
  // co-locate time series with similar values
  // this part is designed to have collisions
  + sortedDimensions.values().limit(SOME_MAX).map(v -> hash32(v)).join() 
  // disambiguating the previous part that may have collisions
  // so that time series with similar values always have a unique _tsid.
  // As we have already added a hash of the sorted dimension keys,
  // we don't need to hash key/value pair, just the values is enough.
  + hash128(sortedDimensions.values()) 
```

Note that this doesn't add any separator bytes on purpose. As Andrew mentioned, they aren't required.
I'm not actually proposing a string concatenation here, this is just to exemplify.
